    "blocks": [
      {"id": "title", "type": "markdown", "body": "# Carparts23 Stage 1 Detector Report"},
      {"id": "technical_summary", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## COCO initialization wins and the checkpoint is ready for transfer\n\nThe COCO-initialized YOLOX-S improved validation AP50:95 from **0.422 to 0.546** under the same 100-epoch protocol. The validation-selected checkpoint then achieved held-out test AP50:95 **0.580**, AP50 **0.737**, AP75 **0.675**, and AR100 **0.779**. Test data was used only after selection. The checkpoint is promoted as `carparts23-yolox-s-coco-v1`; its only taxonomy blocker is the unmeasurable `object` class, which is irrelevant to the configured military transfer groups."},
      {"id": "headline_metrics", "type": "metric-strip", "cardIds": ["test_ap", "test_ap50", "test_ar100", "validation_gain"]},
      {"id": "initialization_findings", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## Initialization improved nearly every measurable class\n\nCOCO initialization improved validation AP for **21 of 22 measurable classes**. The largest gains were tailgate (+28.823 points), trunk (+24.815), hood (+20.054), front door (+18.338), and back bumper (+18.295). Back-right door was the lone regression (-2.705 points), based on only 18 validation instances. The chart is sorted by change; positive bars favor COCO initialization, while the zero line marks parity."},
      {"id": "validation_delta", "type": "chart", "chartId": "validation_delta_chart"},
      {"id": "test_findings", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## Held-out performance is strongest on glass and major body panels\n\nFront glass (91.568 AP), hood (85.339), front door (83.820), and front bumper (82.388) lead the held-out ranking. Front-right light (31.957), wheel (34.498), and front-left light (36.531) are the weakest measurable classes and should anchor future error analysis. Read the chart as absolute held-out class AP, not as a comparison with validation; split composition differs."},
      {"id": "test_ap", "type": "chart", "chartId": "test_ap_chart"},
      {"id": "scope_definitions", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## Scope and metric definitions\n\nThe deterministic source-grouped dataset contains **2,585 train images / 14,210 boxes**, **193 validation images / 1,177 boxes**, and **192 test images / 1,150 boxes**. The split seed is 42 and the source archive SHA-256 is pinned. AP50:95 is mean COCO bounding-box average precision over IoU 0.50 through 0.95; AR100 is mean recall with at most 100 detections per image. Per-class values below are AP percentage points."},
      {"id": "per_class_detail", "type": "table", "tableId": "per_class_table"},
      {"id": "methodology", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## Selection and test methodology preserve the held-out boundary\n\nScratch and COCO-initialized runs used the same prepared split, 100 epochs, batch size 8, mixed precision, 5 warmup epochs, 15 no-augmentation epochs, and evaluation every 5 epochs. The selected checkpoint records best validation AP50:95 0.545746 at epoch 100. One final `--test` evaluation used the standard pycocotools evaluator with no test-driven model or threshold changes. Data checks reconciled split counts, annotation totals, archive provenance, and file hashes before evaluation."},
      {"id": "limitations", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "stage1_evidence", "body": "## The object label is a dataset defect, not a measured detector failure\n\n`object` has only **5 train, 2 validation, and 0 test instances**. Its validation AP is zero and test AP is undefined; COCOeval therefore averages the held-out result over the 22 classes with ground truth. Removing it now would break compatibility with the promoted 23-class head, so it remains as a documented limitation until a versioned dataset/model change. Small samples also make tailgate, trunk, and side-specific door/light AP less stable. These results establish civilian-domain performance only—not military, damage, or operational validity."},
      {"id": "promotion", "type": "markdown", "sourceId": "promotion_lineage", "body": "## The promoted artifact has an immutable identity\n\nVersion: `carparts23-yolox-s-coco-v1`  \nCheckpoint SHA-256: `5ab8c99a5a67ca253aea6f1d4a6974f827b5e5bd16aca227306c129a7fd94d47`  \nStarting COCO checkpoint SHA-256: `f55ded7181e1b0c13285c56e7790b8f0e8f8db590fe4edb37f0b7f345c913a30`  \nCRATER source revision: `f2e18633e3c4640cbc5f98056aead5bdd940e030`  \nYOLOX source revision: `6ddff4824372906469a7fae2dc3206c7aa4bbaee`\n\nThe checkpoint and raw logs remain ignored local artifacts. Their hashes, dataset lineage, evaluation command, and known limitations are preserved in the promotion manifest and tracked Markdown report."},
      {"id": "next_steps", "type": "markdown", "body": "## Stage 2 should start from the promoted parent\n\n1. Collect and annotate licensed military imagery using the six-class taxonomy.\n2. Initialize `yolox_s_crater6.py` from the promoted checkpoint and record its version/hash in every child run.\n3. Report military validation by class, viewpoint, size, and visibility, with special attention to wheel transfer.\n4. Propose Carparts23-v2 to remove or relabel `object`; do not silently change the current taxonomy."},
      {"id": "further_questions", "type": "markdown", "body": "## Questions for the next experiment\n\n- Do weak light, mirror, and wheel results reflect object size, occlusion, label consistency, or visual ambiguity?\n- Does this civilian parent improve military convergence and AP versus official COCO initialization and scratch?\n- Is a 22-class civilian retrain worthwhile after Stage 2 reveals which source classes actually transfer?"}
    ]
  },
